> Aug 19 21:02:34 lyta kernel: avc: ?denied ?{ connectto } for ?pid=13505
> exe=/usr/bin/kdeinit saddr=127.0.0.1 source=8080 daddr=127.0.0.1 dest=34582
> netif=lo scontext=rjc:user_r:user_t tcontext=system_u:system_r:tcp_socket_t
> tclass=tcp_socket

At present, the SELinux module uses the 'tcp_socket' initial SID (with the
system_u:system_r:tcp_socket_t context) in two situations:
1) To label the kernel-created TCP reset socket and any traffic generated
by it.  This should only show up on RST packets, and should never trigger
a 'connectto' permission check.
2) To label traffic generated by a TCP socket that has no user socket
associated with it yet.  This can show up on a SYN ACK from a newly
allocated server socket that has not yet been associated with a user
socket via accept, and trigger this connectto failure.  The server side
would then likely continue trying to send the SYN ACK, and will eventually
succeed after accept is called and we know the actual SID to use for the
server socket.  This is a consequence of the original attempt in LSM to
merely use the inode security field of the user socket for storing
socket security information rather than adding a security field to
the struct sock as in the original SELinux kernel patch [see the module
technical report's discussion of Socket IPC and Networking Changes in
the Changes from the Original SElinux Kernel Patch section].

Since we have recently added a security field to the struct sock and some
new hooks to LSM (Rev 1.348 in BitKeeper), we can likely eliminate the
second case by saving the proper SID in the struct sock when the
tcp_create_openreq_child hook is called and then using this information in
the skb_set_owner_w hook to label the traffic.  However, I think that this
will require passing the listening socket (sk) to the
tcp_create_openreq_child hook in addition to the new socket and the
network buffer, since the SID will be derived from the listening socket.
Wayne?

